# Backend tests

This is the first real test coverage the backend has had — see the July
2026 engineering review, §10, for why that's worth calling out explicitly.

## Approach

Unit-style tests, not integration tests against a real MongoDB. Each test
file mocks the Mongoose models and any external service it touches
(`vi.mock("../models/Submission.js")`, `vi.mock("../controllers/compilerController.js")`
for Judge0, etc.) and calls the exported handler/middleware functions
directly with hand-built `req`/`res`/`next` — no `supertest`, no running
HTTP server. This is intentional:

- It avoids adding a new test-only HTTP dependency for something Express's
  handler functions don't actually need to be invoked.
- It's fast — no DB or network I/O anywhere in the suite.
- It tests the actual decision logic (does this input get accepted or
  rejected, does this middleware call `next()` or short-circuit) rather
  than plumbing.

The tradeoff: it doesn't catch a real Mongoose schema/query bug (a typo'd
field name, a broken index, an actual query that doesn't do what the mock
assumes it does). If/when that tradeoff stops being worth it — most likely
once there's enough business logic that a false-positive from an
over-permissive mock becomes a real risk — the next step is
`mongodb-memory-server` for true integration tests against a real (in
memory) Mongo instance, run as a separate `test:integration` script so the
fast unit suite still runs on every commit.

## Where to start if you're adding the next test

Priority order, per the engineering review:

1. `routes/judge.js` (`submitHandler`) — the grading logic, especially the
   "empty testcases → would silently return Accepted" guard, and the
   Submission-recording behavior (see `routes/judge.test.js`).
2. `routes/progress.js` / `controllers/progressController.js` — the
   solve-verification logic (see `routes/progress.test.js`).
3. `middleware/roleGuard.js` — done, see `middleware/roleGuard.test.js`.
4. `middleware/auth.js` — impersonation swap logic (`req.actingAdminDoc`)
   is security-relevant and still untested.
5. `utils/computeXP.js`, `utils/calculateStreak.js` — pure functions,
   cheap to cover, currently untested.
6. `routes/billing.js` / `routes/billingWebhook.js` — signature
   verification, currently untested.

## Running

```
cd backend
npm install
npm test
```

`npm test` runs `vitest run` (see `package.json`). CI runs this on every
push/PR (see `.github/workflows/ci.yml`) and — as of this change — the
job's `continue-on-error` was removed, so a failing backend test now fails
the pipeline.
